From 2be837b7e289649fcb91a11a6a8bcf00c6069d3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 23:03:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: provide hardware context and selective flow
 invalidation

Expose borrowed conntrack, selected NEIGH route, MTU and accounting context
to hardware adapters without recovering a flow from its opaque cookie.

Allow adapters to request neighbour-aware software fallback and explicit
invalidation handles before admission. Each opted-in flow owns one handle,
shared by both directions. Drivers may retain it with get/put without
pinning a flow, table, conntrack or namespace. Atomic invalidation excludes
cached lookup immediately; native GC performs ordinary flow teardown.
Detaching the Linux flow invalidates its handle too. RCU reclamation
protects lockless lookups, and retained handles cannot name a replacement
flow after tuple or cookie reuse. Handle allocation failure leaves normal
stack forwarding available through the existing flow-add error path.

Native DIRECT tuples cache Ethernet addresses and bypass neighbour output.
Keep routed software tuples on XMIT_NEIGH for opted-in tables and retire
DIRECT tuples built concurrently with binding before packet rewriting.
Both requests are monotonic for the table and survive device unbinding.

Other tables retain their existing behaviour and allocate no handles.
This is a downstream kernel-internal interface; no userspace ABI changes.

Publish a non-vetoing netevent for every committed FIB mutation in either
family, including non-selected IPv4 DSCP/metric aliases and table flushes.
Both FIB notifiers intentionally report only the selected alias and are not
sufficient for complete flow invalidation. Event data is borrowed and holds
only the namespace and destination prefix. The IPv4 event is emitted under
RTNL, which also excludes adapter admission until the mutation and its
route-cache updates finish; the IPv6 event is emitted under the table lock
because router advertisements install and withdraw routes from softirq, so
consumers must be atomic-safe and must revalidate rather than rely on RTNL.

Supply both directional NEIGH destinations to callbacks so admission can
recheck both and reject a generation queued before a change. Each carries
the cookie it was selected with: an IPv6 destination belongs to one FIB
generation and dst_check() rejects every one of them against a zero cookie.

Opted-in flowtables require successful IPv4 FIB lookup for the reverse
route. A forced output interface otherwise allows ip_route_output to
synthesize an on-link unicast route after any FIB error, bypassing route
withdrawal or blackhole policy. Keep that legacy fallback for other callers;
reject flow creation on the experimental path until routing succeeds.
